      "answer": "Here's a Python function that parses a CSV file into a list of dictionaries:\n\n```python\nimport csv\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Union\n\n\ndef parse_csv_to_dicts(\n    file_path: Union[str, Path],\n    delimiter: str = \",\",\n    encoding: str = \"utf-8\",\n) -> List[Dict[str, str]]:\n    \"\"\"Parse a CSV file into a list of dictionaries.\n\n    Each row in the CSV file is converted into a dictionary where the keys\n    are the column headers (from the first row) and the values are the\n    corresponding cell values as strings.\n\n    Args:\n        file_path: Path to the CSV file. Can be a string or a ``Path`` object.\n        delimiter: The delimiter used in the CSV file. Defaults to ``\",\"``.\n        encoding: The encoding used to read the file. Defaults to ``\"utf-8\"``.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of dictionaries, one per data row, mapping column names to\n        their string values.\n\n    Raises:\n        FileNotFoundError: If the given ``file_path`` does not exist.\n        csv.Error: If the file contains malformed CSV data.\n\n",
