      "answer": "# How Photosynthesis Works: A Step-by-Step Guide\n\nPhotosynthesis is the process plants use to convert sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide into glucose (sugar) and oxygen. It happens inside **chloroplasts** \u2014 tiny organelles in plant cells that contain the green pigment **chlorophyll**.\n\nHere's the overall equation:\n\n**6CO\u2082 + 6H\u2082O + light energy \u2192 C\u2086H\u2081\u2082O\u2086 + 6O\u2082**\n\nPhotosynthesis happens in two main stages: the **light-dependent reactions** and the **light-independent reactions** (Calvin cycle).\n\n---\n\n## Stage 1: Light-Dependent Reactions (in the thylakoid membrane)\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Sunlight is absorbed.**\nChlorophyll in the thylakoid membranes absorbs photons (light particles). This excites electrons in chlorophyll to a higher energy level.\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Water is split (photolysis).**\nUsing the absorbed energy, water molecules are split into hydrogen ions (H\u207a), electrons, and oxygen. The oxygen is released as a byproduct \u2014 this is the O\u2082 we breathe.\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 ATP and NADPH are produced.**\nThe excited electrons are passed down an electron transport chain, releasing",
